#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166577 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166967 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "144f097c-0080-5857-b992-8996f194ca22" :knowledge-point/title "Using Indicator Functions to Check Predicates"}] :question/id #uuid "5ebf00aa-406d-5286-b4ae-0f0044ce9b4d" :question/math-academy-id "e-21905" :question/problem "Evaluate the indicator function\n$\\mathbf{1}[9 > 7].$" :question/worked-solution "The indicator function $\\mathbf{1}[P]$ equals $1$ if the predicate $P$ is true and $0$ if $P$ is false.\n![](images/b1/b1a45cb38a9a19c9dac7a7736cb71f2ad0cef5b7026f3eeb6bc573ee11609db4.svg)\n\nHere, the predicate is $9 > 7.$ Since $9$ is greater than $7,$ the predicate is true. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{1}[9 > 7] = 1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166578 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166969 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1900018f-1bac-5623-b45f-e3d9c89f5d74" :knowledge-point/title "Using Indicator Functions in Expressions"}] :question/id #uuid "1687d5ff-6b48-52d9-a947-3425227af366" :question/math-academy-id "e-21906" :question/problem "Consider the function\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\mathbf{1}[x \\geq 1] \\cdot x^2 + \\mathbf{1}[x < 1] \\cdot (-x).\n$$\n\nWhat is $f(-2)?$" :question/worked-solution "The indicator function $\\mathbf{1}[P]$ equals $1$ if the predicate $P$ is true and $0$ if $P$ is false.\n![](images/66/6698e70e8d5cc4c4e4fd3578170ed7e43a2a907e2ab0397352882d87af2d32e1.svg)\n\nFor $x = -2,$ we evaluate each indicator function:\n\n- $\\mathbf{1}[x \\geq 1] = \\mathbf{1}[-2 \\geq 1] = 0$ (since $-2 \\geq 1$ is false)\n\n- $\\mathbf{1}[x < 1] = \\mathbf{1}[-2 < 1] = 1$ (since $-2 < 1$ is true)\n\nSubstituting into the expression for $f(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2) & =1[-2\\ge 1]\\cdot (-2)^{2}+1[-2<1]\\cdot (-(-2)) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 4+1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the indicator functions act as a \"switch\": since $x = -2 < 1,$ the first term vanishes and only the second term contributes to the result."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166579 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166968 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e434dbd-b33c-5997-b3ba-043233cfbc89" :knowledge-point/title "Converting Indicator Notations"}] :question/id #uuid "c831e4d2-736d-5ee1-9c0e-55cee535f361" :question/math-academy-id "e-22020" :question/problem "Let $H = \\{-5, -3, -1, 1\\}.$ Which of the following is equivalent to $\\mathbf{1}_H(0)?$\n\nI. $\\mathbf{1}[0 \\in \\{-5, -3, -1, 1\\}]$\n\nII. $\\mathbf{1}[0 \\notin \\{-5, -3, -1, 1\\}]$\n\nIII. $\\mathbf{1}[H \\in \\{-5, -3, -1, 1\\}]$\n\nIV. $\\mathbf{1}[\\{-5, -3, -1, 1\\} \\in 0]$\n\nV. $\\mathbf{1}[0 = H]$" :question/worked-solution "The indicator function $\\mathbf{1}[P]$ equals $1$ if the predicate $P$ is true and $0$ if $P$ is false.\n![](images/e9/e9658de5d9f87481ee638e6b3aaaa0d628cb4afd415aa73648d71ff3ddd6af00.svg)\n\nRecall that the indicator function $\\mathbf{1}_H(x)$ of a set $H$ equals $1$ if $x \\in H$ and $0$ if $x \\notin H.$ This can be written using Iverson bracket notation as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{1}_H(x) = \\mathbf{1}[x \\in H].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{1}_H(0) = \\mathbf{1}[0 \\in H] = \\mathbf{1}[0 \\in \\{-5, -3, -1, 1\\}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521540 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166967 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "144f097c-0080-5857-b992-8996f194ca22" :knowledge-point/title "Using Indicator Functions to Check Predicates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d31ae15-4799-5dcb-b0de-9552c9af5297" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520788} {:answer/id #uuid "2f80aa26-406f-5364-b5bb-d75a8b6a927a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520864} {:answer/id #uuid "34dba6bf-e41e-5f62-8f3f-cd44ea2f03d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520874} {:answer/id #uuid "61a0a683-cee4-5ee5-8382-168325ade50d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520984} {:answer/id #uuid "beeabc21-ba82-5dc1-b8aa-50cd9eb8eb34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521191}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d31ae15-4799-5dcb-b0de-9552c9af5297" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520788} :answer-field/id #uuid "cbbe48f6-f510-548a-9eb2-bcc76f4b3d7f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521428}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b1a403c5-0788-5593-a233-1f2c8a521c2c" :question/math-academy-id "q-346652" :question/problem "Evaluate the indicator function\n$1[9<3].$" :question/worked-solution "The indicator function $1[P]$ equals $1$ if the predicate $P$ is true and $0$ if $P$ is false.\n\n$$\n1[P]=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is true}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is false}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, the predicate is $9<3.$ Since $9$ is not less than $3,$ the predicate is false. Therefore,\n\n$$\n1[9<3]=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521476 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166967 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "144f097c-0080-5857-b992-8996f194ca22" :knowledge-point/title "Using Indicator Functions to Check Predicates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53f3725f-3e3d-5bae-b981-0ca3ee733a60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520955} {:answer/id #uuid "6d6ff737-46c7-53ae-a062-3f69d9c466f1" :answ